  "query_preview": "..\n(Tomorrow through Monday)\nIssued at 115 PM CDT Mon Aug 24 2026\n\nA lull in activity is expected through much of Tuesday, with \noverall warmer conditions areawide anticipated. The frontal\nboundary will lose some of its definition by Tuesday afternoon, \nbut a small temperature gradient will exist form north to south \nacross the frontal zone. Temperatures in the triple digits can be \nexpected from I-40 south, with upper 90s more common across the \nnorth. Have opted to catch the low hanging fruit with a Heat\nAdvisory for tomorrow south of the boundary. Will hold off\nissuance for now across the north with more uncertainty in\nboundary influences for those locations. The advisory may need to\nbe extended further north with later forecast packages. Potentially\nmore active weather returns by Tuesday night into Wednesday. A \nmore potent mid level shortwave will drop southeast out of the \nHigh Plains. A complex of thunderstorms is forecast to develop \nwell northwest of the forecast area Tuesday evening and track \nsoutheastward through the night Tuesday. This complex may reach \nportions of northeastern Oklahoma early Wednesday morning. \nAdditional showers and storms will be possible during the day \nWednesday, with focus shifting south and east with time as the \nvort max moves across the region. A reinforcing shot of cooler low\nlevel temperatures will also arrive in the wake of this system \nwith more normal temperatures expected Wednesday into Thursday. \n\nUpper ridging is forecast to become more centered over the\nSouthern Plains as we head into the weekend. This will bring \nanother period of hot and dry weather over much of the area.\nTemperatures will trend back well above normal, with little in the\nway of rain chances through the last half of the forecast period. \n\nBowlan",
